{
  "version": 1,
  "format": "blocknote",
  "title": "Choro Brain",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 1 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Choro Brain", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Make the fleet's past understandable and its present intentionally coordinated. Every agent conversation lives in the local state.db database (chat_messages + chat_timeline_events). Choro Brain adds living per-agent summaries, connected context, searchable knowledge, durable memories, and explicit user-directed agent requests on top of that history.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Principles", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Brain maintenance requests and explicitly tagged agent-to-agent deliveries remain visible and auditable in the timeline. Structured connected context stays hidden from the visible message while being clearly delimited as untrusted background in the runtime prompt.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "No new model credentials or integrations. The agent's own session (Claude / Codex / OpenCode) does the summarizing — reuse existing access.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "One summary per agent, updated in place. A living document, never a pile of snapshots.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Content from other agents is untrusted background context — same quarantine discipline as agent-authored memories (memory.rs), clear delimiters, never instructions.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Brain features are always available to every agent. There is no per-agent Brain opt-in switch; the agent's existing access mode still governs what it can do.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Layer 1 — Agent summaries", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Fills the granularity gap between memories (500-char facts) and raw transcripts (100k-token walls): a few hundred words per agent — task, what was done, key decisions, gotchas, files touched, outcome. New summary saves also include a separate plain-text outcome of at most 220 characters for compact weekly display. Existing summaries are not backfilled and keep no outcome until they are updated again.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 3 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "How a summary is created", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "A canned prompt is sent into the agent's own chat and answered in the conversation, but its visible timeline treatment is an app-owned Brain action capsule with the Brain icon — never a user message bubble. The capsule uses a brief one-shot activation animation and keeps the maintenance action auditable without exposing the prompt wall.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "The agent saves the complete replacement through the summary_save MCP tool (the same local-store pattern as memory_save).", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "On update, the prompt tells the agent to call summary_read first (current summary + last covered sequence) and summarize only what's new — never re-tell the whole story. Matters especially after compaction or session restart.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 3 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Triggers", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Terminal: agent moves to Done or Rejected (fired at the transition, while the session is still alive). Also refresh after a ship or solo-lane rejoin — \"what actually landed\" is the most valuable part. Automatic maintenance completions are silently acknowledged by the desktop lane, companion, and notification coordinator, so they never move a user-closed agent back into Needs attention. Suppression is scoped to that exact completion revision; manual summary requests, later replies, failures, and questions retain normal attention behavior.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "No active-work checkpoint: going working → idle, accumulating messages, or pausing between chat turns never triggers an automatic summary. Brain maintenance must not interrupt an active conversation.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Manual: \"Add summary\" / \"Update summary\" button — just presses the same canned-message trigger.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "There is no automatic historical-summary backfill. Older agents without a living summary remain visible in Knowledge; their stored transcripts are retained but are never injected into another agent automatically.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 3 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Storage", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "The agent_summaries table stores agent_id, summary_text, last_summarized_sequence, updated_at, and the legacy edited_by_user marker. The UI treats summaries as generated, read-only knowledge and replaces each agent's row in place.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Staleness check is trivial: max(sequence) in chat_messages > last_summarized_sequence.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 3 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Display", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Rendered as a proper timeline card, styled like the code_review / verification cards — not a raw text reply. Markdown is rendered as headings, paragraphs, emphasis, lists, links, tables, and code rather than exposing raw #, ##, **, or list syntax. One card updates in place for the agent.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Long cards show a Markdown preview with \"Show full summary\" / \"Show less\" controls and keep the explicit \"Update summary\" action. Summary text is not directly editable in the UI.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "The same read-only Markdown summary appears at the bottom of the agent's Notes drawer, with Add summary / Update summary when a refresh is needed.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Injected into the agent's own context on session resume/restart — the agent always comes back knowing what it already did, even after compaction.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Layer 2 — Knowledge: user-directed history", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Knowledge is browsed by the user in Settings. Choro does not inject related past agents into a new chat, display a related-agent badge, or expose agents_search / agent_recall as runtime MCP tools. A model therefore cannot independently select an old agent from inferred similarity.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Retrieval stays intentional: memories are injected under their existing rules, summaries are browsed in Knowledge, and an agent receives another agent's request only after the user explicitly selects that agent in the composer.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Literal SQLite FTS5 virtual tables continue to index real agent titles, summaries, and message bodies, with save/update triggers keeping the index synchronized for local knowledge features. The index is not a trigger and never creates an agent relationship by itself.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Penpot design conversations are excluded because they reuse chat tables but do not have matching real-agent rows.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Agent-to-agent access is explicit-only. Choro does not let a runtime autonomously discover or recall other agents. Project references and connected resources remain pointers only and never grant filesystem permissions.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Every connected indicator shown on an agent — source and linked docs, tasks, designs, and pull requests — is also injected as hidden structured runtime context. Choro refreshes it from stored and derived state on every user turn, marks source resources so \"the doc\" and \"the task\" resolve deterministically, and gives Solo agents both the read-only working snapshot and writable canonical document path.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Layer 3 — Live messaging: # agents and ## projects", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Choro-native request bus: # in the composer opens the agent picker and starts a smart handoff to the agent the user explicitly selected — provider-agnostic, works for Claude, Codex, and OpenCode alike. Pressing Send is the approval: Choro uses the source conversation, living summary, connected context, selected references, and the user's exact note to prepare a concise self-contained brief and delivers it immediately with no second confirmation step. The composer shows only a compact preparing/sending status; generation failure automatically falls back to the unchanged original. There is no general agent_message MCP tool, so a runtime cannot initiate a new cross-agent request by itself. Choro automatically classifies the request as a Question (Ask) or Task (Delegate); explicit action verbs win, questions and explanation requests become Ask, and ambiguous text defaults to Delegate. Ask is a bounded read-only consultation; Delegate may take action under the target's existing access and safety rules. The composer shows the inferred type and lets the user switch it without leaving free-text writing.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Composer grammar stays unambiguous: @ selects files, @@ selects docs and designs, / selects commands, # selects agents, and ## selects projects. The agent picker shows name, status, project, and whether the agent is active; selection inserts a visible agent chip backed by the stable agent ID. The project picker searches every registered project except the current one by name or path; selection inserts a visually distinct Project chip backed by the stable project ID.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "A ## project tag is a message-level pointer, not an access-control feature. At send time Choro resolves the stable project ID to its current path and injects structured project context. The tagged project is reference-only by default unless the user explicitly requests modification, and the agent's existing access mode remains authoritative.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Every explicitly tagged Question or Task wakes the selected target automatically. A stopped backend resumes through the existing safe provider-session path, and a Done agent moves back to In Progress while it handles the request. Incoming requests render once as labeled Question from / Task from cards in the target timeline; the hidden runtime dispatch prompt is never duplicated as a user-message bubble. Outgoing cards show Question to / Task for. Agent names are semibold navigation links in both directions. Historical collision or legacy message rows may remain visible for audit, but never wake an agent.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Every request has a durable return path. The target calls agent_reply once with the request ID when it has a final answer, completion result, or blocker. Choro retries transient local-database locks inside that idempotent write, then routes the response back as an Answer from card in the source conversation. Choro gives that result to the source agent in one hidden continuation so the source can verify, integrate when the original request was a Task, and give the user one coherent final response. Question results remain read-only. The continuation is explicitly forbidden from calling agent_reply or creating another agent request, preventing agent-to-agent loops while still finishing with the user.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Claude Code's native SendMessage / ListAgents (shipped Aug 2026) is an optional optimization for Claude-runtime agents, not the foundation.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Current uses and extensions", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "\"Has anyone been here before?\" — the user searches the project's Knowledge page, then explicitly tags the relevant agent when a live consultation or delegated follow-up is useful.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "No automatic collision radar or similarity-based linking. File overlap and text similarity are too noisy to authorize waking or messaging another agent.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Handoff / resurrection — the user finds a finished agent in Knowledge and explicitly tags it with a Question or Task when a live continuation is wanted.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Cross-project transfer — tag a server, backend, or sibling app with ## so an agent can inspect its implementation and bring the relevant pattern into the current project without pasting paths.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "What changed this week — the new-agent screen lists this project's summaries updated in the last seven days, newest first. Each row shows the saved one- or two-line outcome when present; older summaries without an outcome show only agent identity and time, with no extraction or backfill from their Markdown.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Orchestration — a lead agent tags workers with free-text Questions or Tasks and collects their returned answers, completion results, and blockers in its own conversation.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Memory mining — summaries feed better auto-proposals into the existing memory-proposal flow.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Settings", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Settings exposes Brain as an expandable parent with Knowledge and Memories as nested destinations. Every top-level destination shares one left edge; only Knowledge and Memories are indented, and disclosure arrows appear only on groups that actually contain children.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "bulletListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Knowledge is a read-only summary browser. A project selector defaults to the active project, a search field filters agent titles, workflow status, and summary Markdown, and the selected project's agents appear newest-first with their status and living summary. Long summaries expand in place and render as Markdown. Memories remains the existing separate manager under Brain. There is no Brain enable/disable switch.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": { "level": 2 },
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Implemented architecture", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "agent_summaries storage + summary_save / summary_read MCP tools + the visible canned-prompt action capsule.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Read-only Markdown summary rendering in the timeline, Notes drawer, and Settings → Brain → Knowledge browser.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Automatic refresh only for Done, Rejected, Ship, and Rejoin; no message-count or working → idle trigger. Existing summaries are injected when sessions resume or restart.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "FTS5-backed local indexes retained for the Knowledge surface; autonomous agents_search and agent_recall runtime tools are not exposed.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "No automatic related-past-work block or related-agent chip at agent start.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Explicit # agent Questions and Tasks with one-send contextual brief generation, automatic intent classification, safe wake/resume, durable constrained agent_reply return routing, source-agent synthesis back to the user, and bidirectional navigation links. No general agent_message runtime tool and no collision radar.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "## project references in both new-agent and active-chat composers: searchable picker, stable-ID Project chip, live path resolution, structured prompt context, and saved-message tag.", "styles": {} }]
    },
    {
      "type": "numberedListItem",
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Connected-context bridge: inject DB-backed docs and tasks plus derived designs and resolved pull requests at agent start and on later user turns, with source-role resolution and canonical Solo document paths; keep the metadata hidden from the visible message.", "styles": {} }]
    }
  ]
}
